To reassign all objects owned by the `postgres` user in the `public` schema to the `attacker` role in a PostgreSQL database, you can use the following SQL commands. Make sure you have the necessary privileges to perform these actions.

1. Connect to your PostgreSQL database using a client like `psql`.

2. Run the following SQL commands:

```sql
-- Reassign all tables owned by postgres in the public schema
DO $$ 
DECLARE 
    r RECORD; 
BEGIN 
    FOR r IN (SELECT tablename FROM pg_tables WHERE schemaname = 'public' AND tableowner = 'postgres') 
    LOOP 
        EXECUTE format('ALTER TABLE public.%I OWNER TO attacker;', r.tablename); 
    END LOOP; 
END $$;

-- Reassign all sequences owned by postgres in the public schema
DO $$ 
DECLARE 
    r RECORD; 
BEGIN 
    FOR r IN (SELECT sequencename FROM pg_sequences WHERE schemaname = 'public' AND sequenceowner = 'postgres') 
    LOOP 
        EXECUTE format('ALTER SEQUENCE public.%I OWNER TO attacker;', r.sequencename); 
    END LOOP; 
END $$;

-- Reassign all functions owned by postgres in the public schema
DO $$ 
DECLARE 
    r RECORD; 
BEGIN 
    FOR r IN (SELECT routine_name FROM information_schema.routines WHERE specific_schema = 'public' AND routine_owner = 'postgres') 
    LOOP 
        EXECUTE format('ALTER FUNCTION public.%I() OWNER TO attacker;', r.routine_name); 
    END LOOP; 
END $$;

-- Reassign all views owned by postgres in the public schema
DO $$ 
DECLARE 
    r RECORD; 
BEGIN 
    FOR r IN (SELECT table_name FROM information_schema.views WHERE table_schema = 'public' AND table_owner = 'postgres') 
    LOOP 
        EXECUTE format('ALTER VIEW public.%I OWNER TO attacker;', r.table_name); 
    END LOOP; 
END $$;
```

This script will reassign the ownership of all tables, sequences, functions, and views owned by `postgres` in the `public` schema to the `attacker` role. Make sure to replace `attacker` with the actual role name if it differs. 

